<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ondo State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ondo State is a state in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1976 from the former Western State, with its capital at Akure. Mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, it includes historic kingdoms such as Akure, Owo and Ondo, and its coastal south is home to the Ilaje and Ijaw. It is one of Nigeria's main cocoa-producing states, and Ekiti State was split from it in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ondo-state.jpg" alt="Ondo State" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sasu photography · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IDANRE_HILLS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ondo State lies in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and was created on 3 February 1976 from the former Western State. It borders Ekiti State to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-state">Kogi State</a> to the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a> to the east, Delta State to the south-east, Ogun State to the south-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a> to the north-west, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the south. The coast includes mangrove-swamp forest near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a>. Its climate is tropical wet and dry. The Idanre inselberg hills, over 1,000 metres high, are the highest point in the western half of Nigeria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The state capital, Akure, was formerly the capital of the ancient Akure Kingdom. The population is predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, made up of subgroups known as the Idanre, Akoko, Akure, Ikale, Ilaje, Ondo, Ese Odo, Owo and Ose peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijaw-people">Ijaw people</a>, including the Apoi and Arogbo, live in the south-eastern swamps close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a> border. In Oke-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> town, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a> border, a small population speaks a variant of Yoruba similar to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> dialect.</p><p>The vast majority of the population are Christians, among them members of the Anglican Province of Ondo and Catholics in the Diocese of Ondo, which dates from 1943. Minorities practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The state is divided into 18 local government areas, the major ones being Akoko, Akure, Okitipupa, Ondo, Ilaje, Idanre and Owo, and most citizens live in towns. Its economy rests on cocoa production, farming, asphalt mining and activities connected with its long coastline, and its mineral resources include bitumen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, oil and gas, granite, limestone and kaolin. Universities include the Federal University of Technology Akure, the Adekunle Ajasin University at Akungba Akoko and the Ondo State University of Science and Technology at Okitipupa. Federal highway A121, part of Trans-African Highway 8 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> to Mombasa, crosses the state from Ogun State through Ore to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>, and highway A122 runs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a> through Akure to Edo State.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ondo State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city is now the capital of <b>Ondo State</b>, and the Deji of Akure remains its traditional ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-akure">Kingdom of Akure</a></li><li><blockquote>It is now a town in <b>Ondo State</b>, where the Olowo remains a senior traditional ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-owo">Kingdom of Owo</a></li></ul></section>
